ith her own hands I think it would be  cool to do something with her that ends  up transforming her changes her path  from the assassin mode she's been on for  so long but I'm trying to keep my  expectations in check when it comes to  arias story I would love to see her go  in a different direction but I'm not  sure they're setting that up to this  point number 4 how will Jon react when  he finds out sanso broke her promise to  keep his secret this is a big question  and one I don't have an answer to Jon  should view this as the betrayal that it  is but since these two characters are  separated by geography for the time  being it's hard to say if he'll do  anything when he finds out I do expect  that he'll find out before  episode 5 ends but not sure if Sansa  will experience any immediate  repercussions John has the battle and  his relationship with Daenerys directly  in front of him and I think that  whatever happens between him and Sansa  will be related to the outcome of what  happens in King's Landing personally I  don't think it'll end up getting  completely glossed over though because  it's pretty serious I mean he told her  in confidence they were just all talking  about the importance of family and  sticking together and she turned around  and broke the promise number three why  did John not give ghost a proper goodbye  after episode four concluded Twitter was  flooded with posts about the strange  goodbye between John and ghost like he  couldn't even spend a few seconds  patting his head and telling him he was  a good boy tell him that uncle Tommen  was going to take him up north where he  could run and hunt and have a good life  the answer to this question probably  won't satisfy you if you were one of  those people who lost faith in John a  little bit after the goodbye but the  director of the episode has explained  what's going on here in an interview  with Kim Renfro from insider David  Nutter explained that it all came down  to the way they shot the scene he says  that ghost wasn't right next to the  actors and so forth so there wasn't any  issues with green-screen or situations  where we're dealing with any kind of  rotoscoping when Renfro asked him  directly about if they did it this way  to simplify the filming process Nutr  answered simply with a yes so it is what  it is I guess but I can't help wondering  if John's sending ghosts away to the  north doesn't hint at his own fate there  are quite a few theories out there about  John going up north of Winterfell at the  end of the story so could he end up  following his direwolf when it's all  said and done number two will Daenerys  become the mad queen the idea of Dany  story going in that direction isn't a  new idea this was a theory that book  readers came up with and debated long  before she made her way to Westeros  I've never been a fan of this idea but  after episode 4 it's hard to ignore the  setup that were seeing in episode 4 we  saw Daenerys come to the realization at  the celebration that she's not loved by  the people like John is this leads to  them finally having a conversation where  she can  convinced him to keep his parentage a  secret everything spirals from there  until at the end of the episode she has  lost a dragon lost her closest friend in  Masson day and lost the confidence in  her trusted advisor Varys everything is  pointing her towards a well-deserved fit  of rage it would be hard to blame  Daenerys for burning down King's Landing  based on what we saw her experience in  this episode not to mention everything  she's experienced in her path to get  here but does that actually qualify as  madness for me by definition it doesn't  I mean madness implies severe mental  illness you don't think of a person who  is mad to be conflicted about the things  they're doing they've just lost it I  don't think that in this context that's  what people are actually saying about  Daenerys though I think they're talking  about her becoming the villain 